CASE 02 — Candidate Lifecycle Orchestration & Automation

Context
Candidate experience was inconsistent across roles and hiring managers. Communications lagged. Interviewers often lacked structured context before meetings. Recruiters manually coordinated updates across email and Slack.

The process worked — but it relied heavily on manual follow-up and individual memory.

The Problem
Without structured automation and sequencing, candidates experienced variability. Interviewers weren’t consistently prepared. Recruiters carried unnecessary coordination load. Employer brand impact was dependent on individual effort rather than system design.

Design & Approach
I mapped the entire candidate journey from application to onboarding handoff, identifying friction points and lag moments. I rebuilt candidate communication templates with structured messaging aligned to employer brand positioning.

I implemented automation triggers so candidates received timely emails after each interview, including expectations for next steps. I configured interviewer briefing emails to be delivered automatically each morning, including candidate context and evaluation criteria to improve interview quality.

Slack notifications were structured to alert recruiters and stakeholders at key process milestones. I adjusted sequencing and order of operations to reduce delays between decision and offer generation.

Impact
Candidate communications became consistent and proactive. Interviewers arrived prepared with structured evaluation context. Recruiter coordination time decreased. Decision-to-offer turnaround improved. Withdrawal rates decreased in late-stage processes after take-home assignments were rewritten to be more candidate-friendly.

Why It Matters
Candidate experience and operational efficiency are not separate systems. When workflows are intentionally designed and automated, both improve simultaneously.


